    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "\n### Feature Description\n\nHow does one utilize qTox's spellchecking feature on Windows?  The commit of https://github.com/TokTok/qTox/commit/165449c25493441a363ea8f9274711df8a7e5e1f says that spellchecking was added and appears to show that Hunspell dictionaries are supported, but I am using the latest nightly binaries for Windows, i.e. version v1.18.3-11-gc2d63dae1 commit c2d63dae1346efd558ddaa8aef9f94b7a130bf2e, and the spellchecking feature does not work despite it being checked/enabled under the general settings.\n\nI have placed the two Huntspell *.aff and *.dic dictionary files from LibreOffice that I want to use with qTox in every single place I can think of where they might be called, but misspelled words are never red-underlined or have suggested replacements.\n\nSo, where are the dictionary files supposed to be installed?  Does an environment variable need to be set for qTox to find them, and if so what is the variable name?\n\nAre the standard Hunspell dictionary files in plain text format what qTox expects?  If not, what name(s) and format(s) should the dictionaries be in?\n",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "The dictionary lookup paths can be obtained by calling\n```bash\nhunspell -D\n```\nIf the `hunspell` is not installed, it should can be installed by calling\n```\nwinget install hunspell\n```\n[Here](https://vxlabs.com/2023/11/14/fixing-hunspell-1.7.0-for-emacs-29-on-windows/) this command resulted in the next paths:\n```\nC:\\Users\\%username%\\OneDrive\\Documents\\hunspell\\;C:\\Hunspell\\;%USERPROFILE%\\Application Data\\OpenOffice.org 2\\user\\wordbook;C:\\Program files\\OpenOffice.org 2.4\\share\\dict\\ooo\\;C:\\Program files\\OpenOffice.org 2.3\\share\\dict\\ooo\\;C:\\Program files\\OpenOffice.org 2.2\\share\\dict\\ooo\\;C:\\Program files\\OpenOffice.org 2.1\\share\\dict\\ooo\\;C:\\Program files\\OpenOffice.org 2.0\\share\\dict\\ooo\\\n```\n\n",
            "created_at": "2025-11-30T03:37:27Z",
